plan.text:
{"t":"scope","verdict":"in","reason":"Quadratic graphs and symmetry are core school algebra."}
{"t":"warmup","id":"w1","prompt":"A parabola crosses the x-axis at 2 and 8. Guess: where is its lowest point?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"At x = 5","correct":true},{"id":"b","text":"At x = 8","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"At x = 2","correct":false}]}
{"t":"objective","text":"I'll understand why a parabola's lowest point sits exactly halfway between its roots."}
{"t":"segment","n":1,"title":"Roots on the graph","goal":"Find the roots of a quadratic and see where the curve crosses the x-axis.","representation":"graph","misconception":"Thinking the roots are the vertex's coordinates or where the curve meets the y-axis","check":"tap"}
{"t":"segment","n":2,"title":"Mirror pairs in a table","goal":"See that points the same distance either side of the vertex have equal heights.","representation":"table","misconception":"Thinking a parabola is a lopsided hill with different heights at equal distances","check":"number"}
{"t":"segment","n":3,"title":"Why the middle must be lowest","goal":"Use equal distance d either side of the middle to show the symmetry algebraically.","representation":"worked","misconception":"Thinking (2-d) and (2+d) give different heights because the signs differ","check":"choice"}
{"t":"segment","n":4,"title":"Sliding the roots","goal":"Move a parabola's roots and watch the vertex stay halfway between them.","representation":"graph","misconception":"Halving b without the sign in -b/2a","check":"set"}

segment:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Here is a curve: y equals x squared, minus 10x, plus 16."}
{"t":"math","id":"s1eq","latex":"y = x^2 - 10x + 16","expr":"y = x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Its roots are where it meets the x-axis, and there the height y is zero."}
{"t":"math","id":"s1z","latex":"x^2 - 10x + 16 = 0","expr":"x^2 - 10*x + 16 = 0"}
{"t":"step","id":"s1zf","latex":"(x-2)(x-8) = 0","expr":"(x-2)*(x-8) = 0","why":"two numbers that multiply to 16 and add to -10","from":"s1z"}
{"t":"say","text":"A product is zero only when one bracket is zero, so there are two roots."}
{"t":"solutions","id":"s1r","of":"s1zf","var":"x","values":[2,8],"latex":"x = 2 \\text{ or } x = 8"}
{"t":"say","text":"On the graph, the roots are the two spots where the curve crosses the x-axis."}
{"t":"plot","id":"s1p","x":[-1,11],"y":[-11,18],"fns":[{"id":"s1f","expr":"x^2 - 10*x + 16","label":"y = x² − 10x + 16"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"s1r1","plot":"s1p","at":[2,0],"label":"(2, 0)","on":"s1f"}
{"t":"point","id":"s1r2","plot":"s1p","at":[8,0],"label":"(8, 0)","on":"s1f"}
{"t":"say","text":"Two other spots tempt people: the lowest point, and where the curve meets the y-axis."}
{"t":"point","id":"s1v","plot":"s1p","at":[5,-9],"label":"lowest point","on":"s1f"}
{"t":"point","id":"s1yi","plot":"s1p","at":[0,16],"label":"y-axis crossing","on":"s1f"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s1v","note":"height −9, not 0"}
{"t":"say","text":"Neither is a root, because a root needs height zero, and these sit at heights minus nine and sixteen."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s1yi","note":"height 16, not 0"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here is a fresh curve, y equals x squared, minus 6x, plus 5."}
{"t":"page","title":"Spot the root"}
{"t":"plot","id":"s1q","x":[-1,7],"y":[-5,8],"fns":[{"id":"s1g","expr":"x^2 - 6*x + 5","label":"y = x² − 6x + 5"}]}
{"t":"say","text":"Three points are marked on it, so ask yourself which one has height zero."}
{"t":"point","id":"s1k1","plot":"s1q","at":[3,-4],"on":"s1g"}
{"t":"point","id":"s1k2","plot":"s1q","at":[5,0],"on":"s1g"}
{"t":"point","id":"s1k3","plot":"s1q","at":[0,5],"on":"s1g"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s1q1","text":"Can a curve have just one root?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s1q2","text":"Why does a zero product mean a zero bracket?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s1c","kind":"tap","prompt":"Tap a root of this curve.","figure":"s1q","answer":"s1k2","explain":"Yes: (5, 0) is on the x-axis, so its height is zero. The other two points have heights −4 and 5.","hint":"A root is where the curve touches the x-axis. What is the height there?"}

segment:2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test the curve y equals x squared, minus 10x, plus 16, at points around x equals 5."}
{"t":"table","id":"s2t","head":["x","y"],"rows":[["2","0"],["3","-5"],["4","-8"],["5","-9"],["6","-8"],["7","-5"],["8","0"]],"fn":"x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Look at the heights: they fall to the middle, then rise again in the same order."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s2t","note":"-8, -5, 0 on both sides of 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"A tempting idea is that a parabola is a lopsided hill, so equal distances give different heights."}
{"t":"write","id":"s2w","text":"Lopsided idea: 2 steps left ≠ 2 steps right?","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test it: x equals 3 and x equals 7 are both 2 steps from 5."}
{"t":"math","id":"s2a","latex":"3^2 - 10(3) + 16 = -5","expr":"3^2 - 10*3 + 16 = -5"}
{"t":"math","id":"s2b","latex":"7^2 - 10(7) + 16 = -5","expr":"7^2 - 10*7 + 16 = -5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Both heights are minus 5, so the lopsided idea fails: each side mirrors the other."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"s2w","note":"fails: equal distance, equal height"}
{"t":"say","text":"So any two x-values the same distance from 5 share a height, and the pair needs no new working."}
{"t":"ask","id":"s2q1","text":"Does this work for every parabola?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s2q2","text":"Why is the middle the lowest, not just equal?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s2c","kind":"number","prompt":"The curve y = x² − 10x + 16 has height 7 at x = 1. What is its height at x = 9?","answer":"7","anticipated":[{"value":"-7","misconception":"Thought the far side of the mirror gives the opposite height"},{"value":"9","misconception":"Gave the x-value instead of the height"},{"value":"16","misconception":"Read the height where the curve crosses the y-axis instead of using the mirror partner"}],"explain":"x = 1 and x = 9 are both 4 away from 5, so their heights match.","hint":"How far is x = 1 from 5, and which x is that far on the other side?"}

segment:3.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test the middle, x equals 5, by stepping the same distance d left and right."}
{"t":"page","title":"Why the middle is lowest"}
{"t":"math","id":"s3a","latex":"y = x^2 - 10x + 16","expr":"y = x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Tempting thought: left is 5 minus d, right is 5 plus d, the signs differ, so the heights should differ."}
{"t":"write","id":"s3w","text":"Tempting: −d and +d, so different heights?","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"Don't trust the thought, test it: put x equals 5 minus d into the equation."}
{"t":"math","id":"s3l","latex":"y = (5-d)^2 - 10(5-d) + 16","expr":"y = (5-d)^2 - 10*(5-d) + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Expand the brackets, and watch the minus 10d and plus 10d cancel."}
{"t":"step","id":"s3l2","latex":"y = 25 - 10d + d^2 - 50 + 10d + 16","expr":"y = 25 - 10*d + d^2 - 50 + 10*d + 16","why":"expand the brackets","from":"s3l"}
{"t":"step","id":"s3l3","latex":"y = d^2 - 9","expr":"y = d^2 - 9","why":"collect like terms","from":"s3l2"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now the right side: put x equals 5 plus d into the same equation."}
{"t":"math","id":"s3r","latex":"y = (5+d)^2 - 10(5+d) + 16","expr":"y = (5+d)^2 - 10*(5+d) + 16"}
{"t":"step","id":"s3r2","latex":"y = 25 + 10d + d^2 - 50 - 10d + 16","expr":"y = 25 + 10*d + d^2 - 50 - 10*d + 16","why":"expand the brackets","from":"s3r"}
{"t":"step","id":"s3r3","latex":"y = d^2 - 9","expr":"y = d^2 - 9","why":"collect like terms","from":"s3r2"}
{"t":"say","text":"Same answer both sides: the signs of d cancelled out, so the tempting thought fails."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s3l3","note":"left height"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s3r3","note":"right height, identical"}
{"t":"say","text":"Take d equal to 2: the points at x equals 3 and 7 should be level."}
{"t":"plot","id":"s3p","x":[0,10],"y":[-10,18],"fns":[{"id":"s3f","expr":"x^2 - 10*x + 16","label":"y = x² − 10x + 16"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"s3p1","plot":"s3p","at":[3,-5],"label":"(3, −5)","on":"s3f"}
{"t":"point","id":"s3p2","plot":"s3p","at":[7,-5],"label":"(7, −5)","on":"s3f"}
{"t":"say","text":"Equal heights at equal distances from x equals 5, so the curve turns around right there."}
{"t":"guide","id":"s3g","plot":"s3p","x":5,"label":"x = 5"}
{"t":"line","id":"s3m","plot":"s3p","from":[3,-5],"to":[7,-5],"label":"same height","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"Since d squared is never negative, the height d squared minus 9 is smallest when d is zero."}
{"t":"ask","id":"s3q1","text":"Why does d² make the signs disappear?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s3q2","text":"Would this work for any parabola?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s3c","kind":"choice","prompt":"On y = x² − 10x + 16, compare the heights at x = 5 − d and x = 5 + d.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Equal: both heights are d² − 9","correct":true},{"id":"b","text":"Different, because one has −d and the other has +d","correct":false,"misconception":"Thought opposite signs on d must give different heights, without expanding the brackets"},{"id":"c","text":"The left point is lower, because 5 − d is the smaller x","correct":false,"misconception":"Assumed a smaller x always means a lower height, like reading the curve as a straight slope"},{"id":"d","text":"They differ by 20d, from the leftover d terms","correct":false,"misconception":"Added the −10d and +10d terms instead of letting them cancel"}],"explain":"Right: the d terms cancel, d² ignores the sign, so mirror points are level.","hint":"Expand both brackets and see what happens to the d terms."}

segment:4.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's slide some roots, starting with a curve that crosses the x-axis at 2 and 6."}
{"t":"math","id":"s4e1","latex":"y = (x-2)(x-6)","expr":"y = (x-2)*(x-6)"}
{"t":"plot","id":"s4p","x":[-6,10],"y":[-6,20],"fns":[{"id":"s4f","expr":"(x-2)*(x-6)","label":"y = (x−2)(x−6)"}]}
{"t":"say","text":"Multiplying out gives the form you usually see, with the middle number minus 8."}
{"t":"step","id":"s4e2","latex":"y = x^2 - 8x + 12","expr":"y = x^2 - 8*x + 12","why":"expand the brackets","from":"s4e1"}
{"t":"point","id":"s4r1","plot":"s4p","at":[2,0],"label":"(2, 0)","on":"s4f"}
{"t":"point","id":"s4r2","plot":"s4p","at":[6,0],"label":"(6, 0)","on":"s4f"}
{"t":"say","text":"The fold line sits halfway between the roots, at x equals 4, and the vertex is on it."}
{"t":"guide","id":"s4g1","plot":"s4p","x":4,"label":"x = 4"}
{"t":"point","id":"s4v","plot":"s4p","at":[4,-4],"label":"vertex (4, −4)","on":"s4f"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here is a tempting shortcut: just halve the minus 8 and call that the vertex."}
{"t":"math","id":"s4w","latex":"x = \\frac{-8}{2} = -4","expr":"-8/2 = -4"}
{"t":"guide","id":"s4g2","plot":"s4p","x":-4,"label":"x = −4?"}
{"t":"say","text":"That line lands far left of both roots, so it can't be the fold line."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s4g2","note":"nowhere near the curve's bottom"}
{"t":"say","text":"The fix is the sign: the vertex is at minus b over two, so the minus 8 flips to plus 4."}
{"t":"math","id":"s4m","latex":"x = -\\frac{-8}{2} = 4","expr":"-(-8)/2 = 4"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s4m","note":"the sign flips"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now try a curve you can move: one root stays fixed at 2, and you control the other."}
{"t":"explore","id":"s4x","x":[-2,14],"y":[-30,20],"expr":"(x-2)*(x-a)","params":[{"name":"a","min":3,"max":12,"start":6}],"prompt":"Drag a and watch where the vertex goes compared with the two roots."}
{"t":"say","text":"Use the halfway idea, not a halved number with the wrong sign, and place the vertex where asked."}
{"t":"ask","id":"s4q1","text":"Does the vertex still sit halfway if a root is negative?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s4q2","text":"Why does the sign flip in minus b over two?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s4c","kind":"set","prompt":"Drag a so the vertex sits at x = 5.","explore":"s4x","param":"a","target":[7.8,8.2],"explain":"With roots at 2 and 8, halfway is (2 + 8) ÷ 2 = 5, so the vertex is at x = 5.","hint":"The vertex is halfway between 2 and the other root. Which number is as far above 5 as 2 is below it?"}

reteach:s1c:other.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test an idea with numbers: a root is an x-value that makes y exactly zero."}
{"t":"page","title":"Root means y is zero"}
{"t":"math","id":"r1eq","latex":"y = x^2 - 4x + 3","expr":"y = x^2 - 4*x + 3"}
{"t":"table","id":"r1t","head":["x","y"],"rows":[["0","3"],["1","0"],["2","-1"],["3","0"],["4","3"]],"fn":"x^2 - 4*x + 3"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here the x = 0 row gives y of 3, so crossing the y-axis doesn't make y zero."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r1t","note":"x = 0 gives y = 3, not 0"}
{"t":"say","text":"The lowest row gives y of −1, so the lowest point isn't a root either."}
{"t":"write","id":"r1n","text":"Root: the x-value where y = 0","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"So for any point, ignore how low or how far left it is, and read its height."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"r1n","note":"height must be 0"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now try the checkpoint again, asking of each point: is its height zero?"}

reteach:s2c:a0.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test the idea that the far side of the mirror gives the opposite height, using a different pair of points."}
{"t":"plot","id":"r2p","x":[-1,11],"y":[-20,20],"fns":[{"id":"r2f","expr":"x^2 - 10*x + 16","label":"y = x² − 10x + 16"}]}
{"t":"guide","id":"r2a","plot":"r2p","x":5,"label":"mirror x = 5"}
{"t":"point","id":"r2l","plot":"r2p","at":[0,16],"label":"(0, 16)","on":"r2f"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here x equals 0 is five steps left of the mirror, with height 16, and x equals 10 is five steps right."}
{"t":"line","id":"r2d","plot":"r2p","from":[10,0],"to":[10,-16],"label":"opposite height?","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"If opposite heights were true, the curve would pass through that bottom end, far below the vertex at height −9."}
{"t":"point","id":"r2v","plot":"r2p","at":[5,-9],"label":"vertex, lowest point","on":"r2f"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r2v","note":"nothing can go lower"}
{"t":"say","text":"The vertex is the lowest point, so the mirror side can't dip below it; use that to try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s2c:a1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your answer on a graph: x is where you stand, and y is how high the curve is."}
{"t":"plot","id":"r2p","x":[-1,11],"y":[-10,20],"fns":[{"id":"r2f","expr":"x^2 - 10*x + 16","label":"y = x² − 10x + 16"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"r2a","plot":"r2p","at":[1,7],"label":"x = 1, height 7","on":"r2f"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now stand at x equals 9, which is this vertical line."}
{"t":"guide","id":"r2gx","plot":"r2p","x":9,"label":"x = 9"}
{"t":"say","text":"If the height were also 9, the curve would have to cross this flat line exactly on the x equals 9 line."}
{"t":"guide","id":"r2gy","plot":"r2p","y":9,"label":"height 9?"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r2gx","note":"does the curve meet the flat line here?"}
{"t":"say","text":"Look where the curve actually sits along x equals 9, then try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s2c:a2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"You gave 16, which is the height where the curve meets the y-axis, at x equals 0."}
{"t":"page","title":"Mirror partners on the graph"}
{"t":"plot","id":"r2p","x":[-1,11],"y":[-10,18],"fns":[{"id":"r2f","expr":"x^2 - 10*x + 16","label":"y = x² − 10x + 16"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"r2a","plot":"r2p","at":[0,16],"label":"x = 0, height 16","on":"r2f"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here is that point, next to the one we were given, at x equals 1."}
{"t":"point","id":"r2b","plot":"r2p","at":[1,7],"label":"x = 1, height 7","on":"r2f"}
{"t":"guide","id":"r2g","plot":"r2p","x":5,"label":"fold line x = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"A mirror partner must be the same distance from the fold line, so let's measure both points."}
{"t":"line","id":"r2d1","plot":"r2p","from":[1,7],"to":[5,7],"label":"4 steps","dashed":true}
{"t":"line","id":"r2d0","plot":"r2p","from":[0,16],"to":[5,16],"label":"5 steps","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"The y-axis point is five steps from the fold line, but x equals 9 is only four steps on the other side."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r2d0","note":"different distance, so not the partner"}
{"t":"say","text":"So find the point four steps from the fold line opposite x equals 1, then try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s2c:other.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your thinking on a fresh graph, with the fold line at x equals 5."}
{"t":"plot","id":"r2p","x":[-1,11],"y":[-10,18],"fns":[{"id":"r2f","expr":"x^2 - 10*x + 16","label":"y = x² − 10x + 16"}]}
{"t":"guide","id":"r2g","plot":"r2p","x":5,"label":"fold line x = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Take two points one step either side of the fold line, at x equals 4 and x equals 6."}
{"t":"point","id":"r2a","plot":"r2p","at":[4,-8],"label":"(4, −8)","on":"r2f"}
{"t":"point","id":"r2b","plot":"r2p","at":[6,-8],"label":"(6, −8)","on":"r2f"}
{"t":"line","id":"r2m","plot":"r2p","from":[4,-8],"to":[6,-8],"label":"same height","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"Mirror partners match in height, so now look at the point we were given, four steps left of the fold."}
{"t":"point","id":"r2c","plot":"r2p","at":[1,7],"label":"(1, 7)","on":"r2f"}
{"t":"line","id":"r2d","plot":"r2p","from":[1,7],"to":[5,7],"label":"4 steps left of the fold","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"Picture its mirror partner four steps right of the fold, then try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s3c:b.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your idea with real numbers: start with d equal to 1, 2 and 3, stepping left of 5."}
{"t":"table","id":"r3a","head":["x = 5 − d","y"],"rows":[["4","-8"],["3","-5"],["2","0"]],"fn":"x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now step the same distances right of 5, using the same rule for y."}
{"t":"table","id":"r3b","head":["x = 5 + d","y"],"rows":[["6","-8"],["7","-5"],["8","0"]],"fn":"x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Compare the two tables row by row: does the minus sign on d actually make the heights differ?"}
{"t":"say","text":"A sign inside a bracket gets squared, so check what it really does before guessing; now try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s3c:c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your idea that a smaller x always gives a lower height, using real numbers from this curve."}
{"t":"page","title":"Testing: smaller x, lower height?"}
{"t":"table","id":"r3t1","head":["x","y"],"rows":[["1","7"],["2","0"],["3","-5"],["4","-8"],["5","-9"]],"fn":"x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Read down the table: x gets bigger each row, but look at what the height does."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"r3t1","note":"x grows, y falls"}
{"t":"say","text":"So on this side, the smaller x is actually higher, which breaks the straight-slope idea."}
{"t":"table","id":"r3t2","head":["x","y"],"rows":[["6","-8"],["7","-5"],["8","0"],["9","7"]],"fn":"x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now continue past 5, and compare each row here with a row from the first table."}
{"t":"say","text":"Use what you saw in both tables to try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s3c:d.text:
{"t":"say","text":"I can see why you added those d terms, so let's test your idea with real numbers."}
{"t":"write","id":"r3w","text":"Your idea: gap = 20d, so d = 1 gives a gap of 20","role":"line"}
{"t":"say","text":"Take d equal to 1, 2 and 3, and work out the height at 5 − d and 5 + d."}
{"t":"table","id":"r3t","head":["x","y"],"rows":[["4","-8"],["6","-8"],["3","-5"],["7","-5"],["2","0"],["8","0"]],"fn":"x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Each pair of rows is one d: 4 and 6, then 3 and 7, then 2 and 8."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r3t","note":"compare each pair"}
{"t":"say","text":"Does a gap of 20, 40 or 60 show up anywhere in this table?"}
{"t":"say","text":"Check whether the −10d and +10d really add up, then try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s4c:other.text:
{"t":"say","text":"A guess I often see is that a is the vertex's x-position, so let's test that with numbers."}
{"t":"write","id":"r4n","text":"Roots of (x − 2)(x − a) are 2 and a","role":"note"}
{"t":"table","id":"r4t","head":["a","roots","halfway between"],"rows":[["4","2 and 4","3"],["5","2 and 5","3.5"],["6","2 and 6","4"]]}
{"t":"say","text":"Take a equal to 5: the roots are 2 and 5, and the vertex sits halfway between them."}
{"t":"math","id":"r4m","latex":"\\frac{2+5}{2} = 3.5","expr":"(2+5)/2 = 3.5"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r4m","note":"not 5, so a is not the vertex"}
{"t":"say","text":"So a is a root, not the vertex; the vertex is the middle of 2 and a."}
{"t":"say","text":"Use the table to see how the middle moves, then try the checkpoint again."}

solution:s1c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's work out this curve's roots together, starting with its equation."}
{"t":"page","title":"Finding the roots"}
{"t":"math","id":"sol1eq","latex":"y = x^2 - 6x + 5","expr":"y = x^2 - 6*x + 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"A root is where the curve touches the x-axis, so the height y must be zero."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol1z","latex":"x^2 - 6x + 5 = 0","expr":"x^2 - 6*x + 5 = 0"}
{"t":"step","id":"sol1zf","latex":"(x-1)(x-5) = 0","expr":"(x-1)*(x-5) = 0","why":"two numbers that multiply to 5 and add to −6","from":"sol1z"}
{"t":"say","text":"A product is zero only when a bracket is zero, so the roots are 1 and 5."}
{"t":"solutions","id":"sol1r","of":"sol1zf","var":"x","values":[1,5],"latex":"x = 1 \\text{ or } x = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"On the graph, the point (5, 0) has height zero, so that is the root you tap."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1k2","note":"height 0: a root"}
{"t":"say","text":"An easy slip is picking the lowest point or the y-axis crossing; their heights are −4 and 5, not zero."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s1k1","note":"height −4, not 0"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s1k3","note":"height 5, not 0"}
{"t":"say","text":"Don't worry, this idea will come back in the practice questions, so you'll get another go."}

solution:s2c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's work it through, starting with the rule for this curve."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol2a","latex":"y = x^2 - 10x + 16","expr":"y = x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"step","id":"sol2b","latex":"y = (x-5)^2 - 9","expr":"y = (x-5)^2 - 9","why":"complete the square to show the middle is x = 5","from":"sol2a"}
{"t":"say","text":"The lowest point is at x equals 5, and x equals 1 is four steps to its left."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol2c","latex":"5 - 4 = 1","expr":"5 - 4 = 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"The mirror partner is four steps to the right of 5, which is x equals 9."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol2d","latex":"5 + 4 = 9","expr":"5 + 4 = 9"}
{"t":"say","text":"Mirror partners have the same height, so put 9 into the rule to confirm."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol2e","latex":"(9-5)^2 - 9 = 16 - 9 = 7","expr":"(9-5)^2 - 9 = 16 - 9 = 7"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"sol2e","note":"height at x = 9 is 7, same as at x = 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Your slips were natural: a mirror flips left and right, not up and down, and 9 was the position, not the height."}
{"t":"say","text":"So the height at x equals 9 is 7, and this idea will come back in practice."}

solution:s3c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's expand the brackets, because the signs on d alone can't tell us the height."}
{"t":"page","title":"Worked solution: equal heights"}
{"t":"math","id":"sol3a","latex":"y = x^2 - 10x + 16","expr":"y = x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Start with the left point, x equals 5 minus d, and put it into the equation."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol3l","latex":"y = (5-d)^2 - 10(5-d) + 16","expr":"y = (5-d)^2 - 10*(5-d) + 16"}
{"t":"step","id":"sol3l2","latex":"y = 25 - 10d + d^2 - 50 + 10d + 16","expr":"y = 25 - 10*d + d^2 - 50 + 10*d + 16","why":"expand both brackets","from":"sol3l"}
{"t":"step","id":"sol3l3","latex":"y = d^2 - 9","expr":"y = d^2 - 9","why":"the −10d and +10d cancel","from":"sol3l2"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now the right point, x equals 5 plus d, the same way."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol3r","latex":"y = (5+d)^2 - 10(5+d) + 16","expr":"y = (5+d)^2 - 10*(5+d) + 16"}
{"t":"step","id":"sol3r2","latex":"y = 25 + 10d + d^2 - 50 - 10d + 16","expr":"y = 25 + 10*d + d^2 - 50 - 10*d + 16","why":"expand both brackets","from":"sol3r"}
{"t":"step","id":"sol3r3","latex":"y = d^2 - 9","expr":"y = d^2 - 9","why":"the +10d and −10d cancel","from":"sol3r2"}
{"t":"say","text":"Both heights are d² − 9, so they are equal."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"sol3l3","note":"left height"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"sol3r3","note":"right height, identical"}
{"t":"say","text":"Your thinking was natural: −d and +d do differ, but the d terms cancel once expanded."}
{"t":"write","id":"sol3n","text":"A smaller x is not always lower: the curve turns at x = 5.","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"This mirror idea will come back in the practice questions, so keep it in your pocket."}

solution:s4c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"The roots are 2 and a, and the vertex sits halfway between them, so halfway must equal 5."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol4h","latex":"\\frac{2 + a}{2} = 5","expr":"(2 + a)/2 = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Multiply both sides by 2 to clear the fraction."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol4t","latex":"2 + a = 10","expr":"2 + a = 10","why":"multiply both sides by 2","from":"sol4h"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now take away 2 from both sides to leave a alone."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol4u","latex":"a = 8","expr":"a = 8","why":"subtract 2 from both sides","from":"sol4t"}
{"t":"solutions","id":"sol4s","of":"sol4u","var":"a","values":[8],"latex":"a = 8"}
{"t":"say","text":"The slip is easy to make: a is a root, not the vertex, so the vertex needs the halfway sum."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"sol4h","note":"halfway between 2 and a"}
{"t":"say","text":"So drag a to 8, with roots at 2 and 8; this idea will come back in practice."}

lost:1:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's slow down: a root is just an x-value that makes y equal zero."}
{"t":"page","title":"Roots, slowly"}
{"t":"math","id":"l1k1eq","latex":"y = x^2 - 4","expr":"y = x^2 - 4"}
{"t":"say","text":"Put in some easy numbers for x and see what y comes out as."}
{"t":"table","id":"l1k1t","head":["x","y"],"rows":[["−3","5"],["−2","0"],["−1","−3"],["0","−4"],["1","−3"],["2","0"],["3","5"]],"fn":"x^2 - 4"}
{"t":"say","text":"Two rows have y equal to zero, so those two x-values are the roots."}
{"t":"math","id":"l1k1z","latex":"x^2 - 4 = 0","expr":"x^2 - 4 = 0"}
{"t":"solutions","id":"l1k1s","of":"l1k1z","var":"x","values":[-2,2],"latex":"x = -2 \\text{ or } x = 2"}
{"t":"say","text":"On the graph, y equal to zero means the curve is touching the x-axis."}
{"t":"plot","id":"l1k1p","x":[-4,4],"y":[-5,6],"fns":[{"id":"l1k1f","expr":"x^2 - 4","label":"y = x² − 4"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"l1k1a","plot":"l1k1p","at":[-2,0],"label":"(−2, 0)","on":"l1k1f"}
{"t":"point","id":"l1k1b","plot":"l1k1p","at":[2,0],"label":"(2, 0)","on":"l1k1f"}
{"t":"say","text":"So a root is where the curve meets the axis; now let's go back and spot one."}

lost:2:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's slow down: start at the middle, x equals 5, and step one unit each way."}
{"t":"page","title":"Equal steps from 5"}
{"t":"numberline","id":"l2k1n","range":[2,8],"marks":[{"at":4,"label":"4"},{"at":5,"label":"5 (middle)"},{"at":6,"label":"6"}]}
{"t":"say","text":"Both 4 and 6 are exactly one step from 5, so let's find the height at each."}
{"t":"math","id":"l2k1a","latex":"4^2 - 10(4) + 16 = -8","expr":"4^2 - 10*4 + 16 = -8"}
{"t":"math","id":"l2k1b","latex":"6^2 - 10(6) + 16 = -8","expr":"6^2 - 10*6 + 16 = -8"}
{"t":"say","text":"Same height, minus 8, on both sides. Now try two steps away, at 3 and 7."}
{"t":"table","id":"l2k1t","head":["x","y"],"rows":[["3","-5"],["4","-8"],["6","-8"],["7","-5"]],"fn":"x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"l2k1t","note":"equal steps from 5, equal heights"}
{"t":"say","text":"Each pair matches, so steps of the same size either side of 5 give the same height; now back to your question."}

lost:3:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"No worries, let's do it with plain numbers first, using the same curve and the middle at x equals 5."}
{"t":"page","title":"Same distance, same height"}
{"t":"math","id":"l3k1a","latex":"y = x^2 - 10x + 16","expr":"y = x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Take one step of 1 to the left of 5, which is x equals 4, and work out the height."}
{"t":"math","id":"l3k1b","latex":"4^2 - 10(4) + 16 = 16 - 40 + 16 = -8","expr":"4^2 - 10*4 + 16 = 16 - 40 + 16 = -8"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now one step of 1 to the right, x equals 6."}
{"t":"math","id":"l3k1c","latex":"6^2 - 10(6) + 16 = 36 - 60 + 16 = -8","expr":"6^2 - 10*6 + 16 = 36 - 60 + 16 = -8"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"l3k1c","note":"same height as x = 4"}
{"t":"say","text":"Same distance from 5, same height. Let's try a step of 2 to see if it keeps happening."}
{"t":"table","id":"l3k1t","head":["x","distance from 5","y"],"rows":[["3","2 left","-5"],["7","2 right","-5"],["4","1 left","-8"],["6","1 right","-8"]],"fn":"x^2 - 10*x + 16"}
{"t":"say","text":"Each pair matches, and the letter d in the board work just stands for any step size, like 1 or 2."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"l3k1t","note":"equal steps, equal heights"}
{"t":"say","text":"Have another look at the algebra on the earlier page, then try the question again."}

lost:4:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Forget curves for a moment: what number sits exactly halfway between 2 and 8?"}
{"t":"numberline","id":"l4k1n","range":[0,12],"marks":[{"at":2,"label":"2"},{"at":5,"label":"halfway"},{"at":8,"label":"8"}]}
{"t":"math","id":"l4k1a","latex":"\\frac{2+8}{2} = 5","expr":"(2+8)/2 = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now slide both ends right by 2, to 4 and 10, and the halfway point slides right by 2 as well."}
{"t":"math","id":"l4k1b","latex":"\\frac{4+10}{2} = 7","expr":"(4+10)/2 = 7"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here are three pairs of roots, each with its halfway point."}
{"t":"table","id":"l4k1t","head":["Left root","Right root","Halfway"],"rows":[["2","8","5"],["4","10","7"],["6","12","9"]]}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"l4k1t","note":"the halfway column follows the roots"}
{"t":"say","text":"The vertex sits at that halfway number, because the curve is a mirror image across it."}
{"t":"say","text":"So when you drag the slider, the roots move, and the vertex simply follows their middle; have another go."}

answer:s1q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Yes, it can: the two roots slide together until they become one."}
{"t":"guide","id":"a1g","plot":"s1p","x":5,"label":"fold line x = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Our roots 2 and 8 are mirror partners around 5, so imagine both moving in toward 5."}
{"t":"math","id":"a1m","latex":"x^2 - 10x + 25 = 0","expr":"x^2 - 10*x + 25 = 0"}
{"t":"step","id":"a1s","latex":"(x-5)^2 = 0","expr":"(x-5)^2 = 0","why":"a perfect square","from":"a1m"}
{"t":"say","text":"A square is zero only when x minus 5 is zero, so x equals 5 is the only root."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"a1s","note":"one repeated root"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here the lowest point touches the x-axis instead of crossing it; where would that touching point sit for roots both at 5?"}

answer:s1q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Look at our line (x − 2)(x − 8) = 0: two brackets multiplied together make zero."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s1zf","note":"two numbers multiplied"}
{"t":"say","text":"Try it with numbers: if 5 times something is 0, that something has to be 0."}
{"t":"math","id":"a2n","latex":"5 \\times \\square = 0","expr":"5*0 = 0"}
{"t":"say","text":"Multiplying by any number other than zero never gives zero, so at least one factor must already be zero."}
{"t":"say","text":"Say x is 5: the brackets become 3 and −3, and 3 times −3 is −9, not zero."}
{"t":"math","id":"a2m","latex":"(5-2)(5-8) = 3 \\times (-3) = -9","expr":"(5-2)*(5-8) = 3*(-3) = -9"}
{"t":"say","text":"Neither bracket was zero, so the product wasn't. Does that make sense of why only x = 2 or 8 work?"}

answer:s2q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Yes, every parabola is its own mirror image, whatever numbers are in its equation."}
{"t":"guide","id":"a3ax","plot":"s1p","x":5,"label":"mirror line x = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Any parabola can be written like this, and its mirror line always sits at minus b over 2a."}
{"t":"math","id":"a3g","latex":"y = ax^2 + bx + c","expr":"y = a*x^2 + b*x + c"}
{"t":"math","id":"a3m","latex":"x = \\frac{-b}{2a} = \\frac{-(-10)}{2(1)} = 5","expr":"-(-10)/(2*1) = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"When there are roots, that mirror line is exactly halfway between them, like our 2 and 8."}
{"t":"line","id":"a3l","plot":"s1p","from":[2,0],"to":[8,0],"label":"halfway is 5","dashed":true}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1v","note":"on the mirror line"}
{"t":"say","text":"Some parabolas never touch the x-axis, so they have no roots to halve, but they are still symmetric."}
{"t":"write","id":"a3n","text":"No roots? Still a mirror line, at x = −b ÷ 2a","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"Try this: if a parabola has roots at 1 and 7, where is its mirror line?"}

answer:s2q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Equal heights only show the fold line; to prove the middle is lowest, we measure each height from the middle."}
{"t":"guide","id":"a4g","plot":"s1p","x":5,"label":"x = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Take a point d steps from 5, and put it into the equation."}
{"t":"math","id":"a4h","latex":"(5+d)^2 - 10(5+d) + 16 = d^2 - 9","expr":"(5+d)^2 - 10*(5+d) + 16 = d^2 - 9"}
{"t":"say","text":"Going d steps left gives the same d² − 9, so both sides match."}
{"t":"write","id":"a4w","text":"d² is never negative, so y = d² − 9 is never below −9","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"Only d = 0 makes d² zero, so the bottom height of −9 happens at the middle alone."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1v","note":"d = 0: lowest"}
{"t":"say","text":"So where would the lowest point be if the height were d² + 4 instead, and what would that height be?"}

answer:s3q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Squaring means multiplying a number by itself, and a negative times a negative is positive."}
{"t":"math","id":"a5a","latex":"(-2)^2 = 2^2 = 4","expr":"(-2)^2 = 2^2 = 4"}
{"t":"say","text":"So a step of minus d and a step of plus d give the same square."}
{"t":"math","id":"a5b","latex":"(-d)^2 = d^2","expr":"(-d)^2 = d^2"}
{"t":"say","text":"In the left working, the minus 10d and plus 10d terms flip sign, but they cancel each other."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"s3l2","note":"−10d and +10d cancel"}
{"t":"say","text":"The right side cancels the same way, so only d², which ignores the sign, is left."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"s3r2","note":"+10d and −10d cancel"}
{"t":"say","text":"Does that make sense? What is the height difference between a step of minus 3 and plus 3?"}

answer:s3q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Yes, for every parabola, because any quadratic can be rewritten with its middle point h and height k."}
{"t":"math","id":"a6g","latex":"y = a(x-h)^2 + k","expr":"y = a*(x-h)^2 + k"}
{"t":"say","text":"Step d to the left of h, and the bracket squares to d squared."}
{"t":"math","id":"a6l","latex":"y = a(h-d-h)^2 + k = ad^2 + k","expr":"a*(h-d-h)^2 + k = a*d^2 + k"}
{"t":"say","text":"Step d to the right and you get exactly the same height."}
{"t":"math","id":"a6r","latex":"y = a(h+d-h)^2 + k = ad^2 + k","expr":"a*(h+d-h)^2 + k = a*d^2 + k"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"a6r","note":"same as the left side"}
{"t":"say","text":"On our board, h is 5, so the fold line is x equals 5."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"s3g","note":"h = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"If a is negative, the parabola flips, so this middle is the highest point instead."}
{"t":"say","text":"So in y equals a(x − h)² + k, which part makes the left and right heights match?"}

answer:s4q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Yes, it still works, because the halfway rule only uses the two roots, whatever their signs."}
{"t":"math","id":"a7e1","latex":"y = (x+2)(x-4)","expr":"y = (x+2)*(x-4)"}
{"t":"say","text":"This curve has roots at negative 2 and 4, so we add them and halve."}
{"t":"math","id":"a7m","latex":"\\frac{-2+4}{2} = 1","expr":"(-2+4)/2 = 1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Check it: x equals 0 and x equals 2 are both one step from 1, so their heights should match."}
{"t":"math","id":"a7h1","latex":"(0+2)(0-4) = -8","expr":"(0+2)*(0-4) = -8"}
{"t":"math","id":"a7h2","latex":"(2+2)(2-4) = -8","expr":"(2+2)*(2-4) = -8"}
{"t":"say","text":"Both heights are negative 8, so the mirror line is at x equals 1, even with a negative root."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"a7m","note":"add the roots, then halve"}
{"t":"say","text":"Does that make sense? Try it yourself: where would the mirror line be for roots at negative 6 and 2?"}

answer:s4q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Multiply out the brackets from our board and watch where the roots end up."}
{"t":"math","id":"a8e1","latex":"(x-2)(x-6) = x^2 - (2+6)x + 2\\times 6","expr":"(x-2)*(x-6) = x^2 - (2+6)*x + 2*6"}
{"t":"say","text":"Each bracket subtracts a root, so the x term is minus the roots' total, and b equals minus 8."}
{"t":"math","id":"a8e2","latex":"b = -(2+6) = -8","expr":"-(2+6) = -8"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"a8e2","note":"b is minus the roots' total"}
{"t":"say","text":"Halfway between the roots is their total over two, which is minus b over two."}
{"t":"math","id":"a8e3","latex":"\\frac{2+6}{2} = \\frac{-(-8)}{2} = 4","expr":"(2+6)/2 = -(-8)/2 = 4"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"s4m","note":"the flip comes from b being minus the total"}
{"t":"say","text":"So the sign flips because b holds the roots' total with a minus; if roots total 10, what is b?"}

recap.text:
{"t":"idea","id":"i1","text":"Roots are where the curve crosses the x-axis, where y = 0.","seg":1}
{"t":"idea","id":"i2","text":"Points the same distance either side of the vertex have equal heights.","seg":2}
{"t":"idea","id":"i3","text":"Stepping d left or right of the middle gives the same height, so the curve is symmetric.","seg":3}
{"t":"idea","id":"i4","text":"The vertex sits halfway between the roots, and it moves with them.","seg":4}
{"t":"say","text":"You guessed x = 5 for roots at 2 and 8, so what makes that guess right now?"}
{"t":"check","id":"rp1","kind":"number","idea":"i4","prompt":"A parabola crosses the x-axis at x = −3 and x = 9. What is the x-coordinate of its lowest point?","answer":"3","anticipated":[{"value":"6","misconception":"Added the roots but forgot to divide by 2"},{"value":"-3","misconception":"Picked one of the roots instead of the point halfway between them"}],"explain":"Halfway between −3 and 9 is (−3 + 9) ÷ 2 = 3.","hint":"The lowest point is exactly midway between the two roots."}
{"t":"check","id":"rp2","kind":"choice","idea":"i2","prompt":"A parabola has its vertex at x = 5. The point at x = 2 has height 7. What is the height at x = 8?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"7","correct":true,"value":"7"},{"id":"b","text":"10","correct":false,"misconception":"Thought the height keeps rising by the same amount as you move right","value":"10"},{"id":"c","text":"8","correct":false,"misconception":"Gave the x-value instead of the height","value":"8"},{"id":"d","text":"5","correct":false,"misconception":"Gave the vertex's x-value instead of a height","value":"5"}],"explain":"x = 2 and x = 8 are both 3 from the vertex, so their heights match.","hint":"How far is each of x = 2 and x = 8 from x = 5?"}
{"t":"check","id":"rp3","kind":"number","idea":"i1","prompt":"What is the lowest value of y = x² − 8x + 12?","answer":"-4","anticipated":[{"value":"4","misconception":"Gave the vertex's x-coordinate instead of its height"},{"value":"12","misconception":"Read the y-axis crossing as the lowest point"}],"explain":"The roots are 2 and 6, the vertex is at x = 4, and y = 16 − 32 + 12 = −4.","hint":"Find the roots, go halfway, then put that x back into the equation."}
